{
  "slug": "cheapest-flights-within-k-stops",
  "title": "Cheapest Flights Within K Stops",
  "topic": "graphs",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "layered Bellman-Ford / BFS on the number of flights taken",
  "statement": "A traveller needs to get from city `src` to city `dst`. There are `n` cities numbered `0` to `n - 1`, and `flights` lists the available legs, `flights[i] = [from, to, price]` being a direct flight that costs `price`. Every leg joins two of those `n` cities, so `from` and `to` always lie in `0 .. n - 1`, and `src` and `dst` are city ids in the same range. The traveller has agreed not to make more than `k` intermediate stops, so an itinerary may use at most `k + 1` flights; every stop counts, including one in a city already visited.\n\nReturn the cheapest total price of an itinerary from `src` to `dst` that respects the stop limit, or `-1` when no such itinerary exists. When `src` equals `dst` the price is `0`, because no flight is needed at all. All prices are positive, so an itinerary that revisits a city is never cheaper than the one that skips the detour.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= n <= 8`, `0 <= flights.length <= 25`, `0 <= flights[i][0] <= n - 1`, `0 <= flights[i][1] <= n - 1`, `1 <= flights[i][2] <= 10`, `0 <= src <= n - 1`, `0 <= dst <= n - 1`, `0 <= k <= 8`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "n = 1, flights = [], src = 0, dst = 0, k = 0",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "n = 2, flights = [], src = 1, dst = 0, k = 3",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(n: number, flights: number[][], src: number, dst: number, k: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The limit is on flights taken, not on cities seen, so the cheapest fare depends on both the city and the hop count you arrive with.",
    "Enumerate itineraries with a depth-limited search from src and notice how often the same city is re-solved at the same hop count.",
    "Keep the best price per city after exactly e flights. Extend layer e to layer e + 1 by relaxing each flight out of the old layer's values, and remember the copy so one pass cannot use two flights."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force walks itineraries directly: a depth-limited DFS from `src` that keeps the cities on the current route in a visited set, refuses to go past `k + 1` flights, and records the cheapest arrival at `dst`, with a branch-and-bound cut that abandons a route once its cost reaches the best answer found so far. Positive prices mean an optimal itinerary is simple, so refusing to revisit a city loses nothing. The optimal version stops enumerating routes and keeps only the cheapest price for each (city, flights used) pair. Start with `dist[src] = 0` and relax every flight from the previous layer's distances into a fresh copy of the array, `k + 1` times. That is Bellman-Ford restricted to hop layers, which is exactly a BFS over the number of flights taken.",
    "why_optimal": "The cheap answer is cheap because of the state space. Plain Dijkstra fails here because the cheapest route to a city may use too many flights, so the state has to carry the hop count. The layered relaxation touches each flight once per layer, at most 9 * 25 = 225 relaxations against a 25-edge input, and needs only two arrays of `n` numbers. The DFS instead explores a number of itineraries that grows factorially with `n`, repeating the same sub-route over and over.",
    "pitfalls": "Relaxing into `dist` itself instead of into a copy: one pass then chains several flights together and silently permits more stops than `k`. Looping `k` times instead of `k + 1` times, which earns `k` flights rather than `k` stops and rejects valid routes. Returning `dist[dst]` while it is still infinite rather than `-1`. Forgetting that `src == dst` is a legal zero-cost itinerary. Indexing the distance array with a city id taken from a flight without first checking that the id lies in `0 .. n - 1`: the read yields `undefined`, `undefined + price` is not a number, and every comparison against it is false, so such a leg must be dropped rather than relaxed. In the DFS, marking a city visited before checking the stop limit, or unmarking it on the way out after an early return, leaves the visited set inconsistent and drops reachable routes."
  },
